        "original_id": "9b40b842-494c-564f-b497-377969da3c37",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer, A Charlie Brown Christmas, A Kidnapped Santa Claus\n- Companies: Rankin/Bass Productions, Universal Television, NBC, CBS, General Electric, Bell Canada\n- People: Johnny Marks, Robert L. May, Lee Mendelson, Bill Melendez, Larry D. Mann, L. Frank Baum\n- Concepts: Christmas television specials, stop motion animation, Christmas mythology\n\nFacts:\n- In 1939, Robert L. May wrote the poem \"Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer.\"\n- Johnny Marks wrote the song \"Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer\" based on Robert L. May's 1939 poem of the same name.\n- \"Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer\" is a 1964 Christmas stop motion animated television special produced by Rankin/Bass Productions and currently distributed by Universal Television.\n- \"Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer\" first aired on NBC in the United States on Sunday, December 6, 1964, with General Electric as sponsor under \"The General Electric Fantasy Hour.\"\n- \"A Charlie Brown Christmas\" was produced by Lee Mendelson, directed by Bill Melendez, and debuted on CBS on December 9, 1965.\n- In the 1980s, Larry D. Mann was best known as \"The Boss\" in a series of Bell Canada television commercials.\n- Larry D. Mann voiced Yukon Cornelius in \"Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer.\"\n- L. Frank Baum wrote the Christmas-themed short story \"A Kidnapped Santa Claus,\" which has been described as one of his most beautiful stories and as an influential contribution to Christmas mythology.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "9245aa41-027f-5a1b-9243-47e5f56d259d",
        "text": "Page: Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer (TV special). Sentence: Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer is a 1964 Christmas stop motion animated television special produced by Rankin/Bass Productions and currently distributed by Universal Television.\nPage: Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer (TV special). Sentence: It first aired Sunday, December 6, 1964, on the NBC television network in the United States, and was sponsored by General Electric under the umbrella title of \"The General Electric Fantasy Hour\".\nPage: Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer (TV special). Sentence: The special was based on the Johnny Marks song \"Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer\" which was itself based on the poem of the same name written in 1939 by Marks' brother-in-law, Robert L. May.\nPage: A Charlie Brown Christmas. Sentence: Produced by Lee Mendelson and directed by Bill Melendez, the program made its debut on CBS on December 9, 1965.\nPage: Larry D. Mann. Sentence: He was best known as \"The Boss\" in a series of Bell Canada television commercials in the 1980s and for voicing the character of Yukon Cornelius in \"Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer\".\nPage: A Kidnapped Santa Claus. Sentence: \"A Kidnapped Santa Claus\" is a Christmas-themed short story written by L. Frank Baum, famous as the creator of the Land of Oz; it has been called \"one of Baum's most beautiful stories\" and constitutes an influential contribution to the mythology of Christmas.",
